1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,304
This is Crossfade, a next-market radar for African music.

2
00:00:03,354 --> 00:00:09,894
It tells labels and distributors which country each rising
song is likely to chart in next, four weeks before it

3
00:00:09,944 --> 00:00:13,012
happens, and it shows the evidence behind every pick.

4
00:00:13,721 --> 00:00:20,091
A distributor's marketing team can only work a handful of
markets each week, but there are forty-three national

5
00:00:20,141 --> 00:00:20,496
charts.

6
00:00:20,546 --> 00:00:25,817
Today those choices are mostly gut feel, so effort often
lands where a song was never going.

7
00:00:26,525 --> 00:00:32,646
This is the live radar for the chart week of the fifteenth
of October, filtered to songs charting in Africa.

8
00:00:32,696 --> 00:00:34,818
Let Me Be is charting only in Nigeria.

9
00:00:34,868 --> 00:00:41,389
Crossfade ranks Tanzania first, at twenty-two percent,
because songs often travel from Nigeria to Tanzania, and the

10
00:00:41,439 --> 00:00:44,932
lead artist has already charted there with nine earlier
songs.

11
00:00:44,982 --> 00:00:46,589
Every pick shows its reasons.

12
00:00:47,296 --> 00:00:55,027
To test it honestly, the model was trained on data up to the
end of 2024, and then scored once on 2025 and 2026.

13
00:00:55,077 --> 00:00:59,404
The backtest puts each prediction next to what really
happened.

14
00:00:59,454 --> 00:01:03,086
The ticks mark the markets the song actually entered.

15
00:01:03,736 --> 00:01:05,092
Underneath are corridors.

16
00:01:05,142 --> 00:01:10,549
For every pair of countries, how often a song charting in
one enters the other within four weeks.

17
00:01:10,599 --> 00:01:15,612
They are re-estimated every quarter using only the past, so
nothing leaks from the future.

18
00:01:16,262 --> 00:01:19,020
Here is the result that matters for a budget.

19
00:01:19,070 --> 00:01:23,014
Each week, every model picks its ten best song and market
pairs.

20
00:01:23,064 --> 00:01:26,072
Forty-one percent of Crossfade's picks came true.

21
00:01:26,122 --> 00:01:28,942
The best simple rule managed thirteen percent.

22
00:01:29,592 --> 00:01:36,155
It also beat every baseline in each validation year, and its
probabilities are calibrated, so a twenty percent chance

23
00:01:36,205 --> 00:01:38,077
really means about twenty percent.

24
00:01:38,783 --> 00:01:43,668
Crossfade forecasts where songs spread on their own, not
what promotion adds.

25
00:01:43,718 --> 00:01:47,257
The next step is testing that with distributor partners.

26
00:01:47,307 --> 00:01:49,244
Crossfade, by Lindokuhle Nyoka.

